{
  "scenario": {
    "id": "P10",
    "title": "Flash vs SRAM - the same loop timed with the cycle counter, with and without the XIP cache",
    "layer": "Experiments",
    "description": "A 16-instruction checksum loop runs 200 iterations. main first reads DWT CYCCNT twice back to back to measure what a measurement costs, then reads it before and after the call and prints both numbers. Three variants place the loop in flash (through the XIP cache), in flash with the cache disabled (every fetch pays the miss penalty), and in SRAM. The data table is always in SRAM, so only instruction fetches move between regions.\n",
    "program": "bench",
    "options": {
      "iterations": 200
    },
    "config": {
      "seed": 1,
      "max_cycles": 400000
    },
    "variants": [
      {
        "name": "flash_cached",
        "options": {
          "code_region": "flash"
        },
        "config": {
          "xip_cache_enabled": true
        }
      },
      {
        "name": "flash_uncached",
        "options": {
          "code_region": "flash"
        },
        "config": {
          "xip_cache_enabled": false
        }
      },
      {
        "name": "sram",
        "options": {
          "code_region": "sram"
        }
      }
    ],
    "expected": {
      "summary": "sram is fastest, flash_cached is within a few percent of it (only cold misses), flash_uncached is several times slower; the instruction count is identical in all three.",
      "checks": [
        {
          "check": "no_faults",
          "variant": "sram"
        },
        {
          "check": "uart_contains",
          "variant": "sram",
          "text": "bench cycles="
        },
        {
          "check": "variant_lt",
          "metric": "bench.bench_cycles",
          "a": "sram",
          "b": "flash_cached"
        },
        {
          "check": "variant_lt",
          "metric": "bench.bench_cycles",
          "a": "flash_cached",
          "b": "flash_uncached"
        },
        {
          "check": "variant_gt",
          "metric": "bench.xip_misses",
          "a": "flash_uncached",
          "b": "flash_cached"
        }
      ]
    },
    "what_to_look_for": "summary.variants.<name>.bench: bench_cycles printed by the program and xip_hits/xip_misses. flash_cached has a handful of misses (the loop fits in a few cache lines; the first pass misses, the other 199 hit), so its bench_cycles is only slightly above sram. flash_uncached has a miss per fetch and bench_cycles several times higher. instret and static_code_bytes are identical in all three: the program is the same, only where the fetch comes from changes. The UART lines \"bench cycles=\" and \"overhead=\" are what the student prints on the board; bench_cycles_corrected subtracts the overhead (3 cycles here: one load from the PPB).\n",
    "likely_root_causes": [
      "Code in flash running slower than expected: the loop is larger than the XIP cache, or the cache was disabled, or the flash clock divider is high.",
      "SRAM copy not faster than flash: the loop already fits in the cache, so the difference is only the cold misses; or the function was not really placed in SRAM (check the map file).",
      "Timing noise: an interrupt fired during the measurement; measure with interrupts off or repeat and take the minimum."
    ],
    "how_to_debug": [
      "Use objdump or the map file to confirm the function's address: 0x10xxxxxx is flash, 0x20xxxxxx is SRAM (use __attribute__((section(\".ram_code\"))) or the linker script).",
      "Read DWT CYCCNT before and after; measure an empty call first and subtract that overhead.",
      "Disable the XIP cache (RP2350 XIP_CTRL) and repeat: the slowdown is the cache's contribution.",
      "Vary the loop size until it exceeds the cache; the flash number jumps while the SRAM number stays linear."
    ],
    "what_this_means": "Instruction fetch cost depends on where the code lives and whether a cache is hiding the slow memory. A small hot loop is almost free from flash because the cache hits after the first pass; a large or scattered program is not. Cycle counts, not milliseconds, make this visible, and the same instruction count with different cycle counts is the definition of a memory-hierarchy effect.\n",
    "hidden_symptom": "The same function runs faster when moved to RAM, but only a little, unless the cache is off."
  },
